{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787057507,"nonce":"ZYCytnShmoOn0X8jAWGHKxrzQQ0=","title":"`suppressed` is a State value that swallows `firing`, so a silenced alert stops being known to be firing","message":"**What is wrong.** `alerts.state` is one of `firing | suppressed | resolved | expired`, so `suppressed`\noccupies the slot `firing` would use. Suppression is not a phase of a signal's life — it is a statement\nabout whether Alertmanager is delivering it — and it is orthogonal to whether the signal is firing.\nThe same product already knows this: `internal/alerts/domain/snooze.go:25-27` refuses to let snooze\nbecome a State because \"an alert can be firing AND acked AND snoozed at once, and all three are\ndisplayed\". Suppression is the same kind of fact and got the opposite treatment.\n\n**The demonstration.** `Case.AlertState()` (`internal/alerts/domain/case.go:298-308`) tests suppression\nFIRST and returns `StateSuppressed`; `StateFiring` is unreachable for a silenced open episode. That one\nvalue is written to `alerts.state` by `setProjectionBatchSQL` (`internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:1004-1016`).\nSo every reader asking `state = 'firing'` silently misses every alert that is firing while silenced:\n`internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:926-929`, `internal/grouping/repository/member.go:261-262`,\n`internal/stats/repository/stats.go:232`. The schema has been carrying the workaround since 00007 —\n`alerts_open_idx` cannot say \"live\" as `state = 'firing'` and spells it as a disjunction\n(`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:77-78`).\n\n**Why it matters.** A silence is the most common thing an operator does to a firing alert, so the\nalerts this loses are not an edge case. A dashboard counting firing alerts under-reports during exactly\nthe window somebody has silenced something, and \"is anything still on fire?\" cannot be answered from\nthe column whose job is to answer it. Every consumer that has already worked around this has encoded\nthe workaround differently, so the disagreement is now spread across three modules and one index.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:56,77-78` · `internal/alerts/domain/case.go:298-308` ·\n`internal/alerts/domain/enums.go:239-245` · `internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:926-929,1004-1016` ·\n`internal/grouping/repository/member.go:261-262` · `internal/stats/repository/stats.go:232` ·\n`internal/alerts/domain/snooze.go:25-32` (the reasoning to follow) · `docs/design/SPEC.md` §B.1, §B.3.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.**\n1. `alerts_state_ck` admits `firing | resolved | expired` and nothing else.\n2. `suppression_reason` and `suppressed_by` are columns on `alerts`, independent of `state`, and an\n   alert that is firing while silenced reads `state = 'firing'` with a non-null `suppression_reason`.\n3. `Case.AlertState()` no longer consults suppression.\n4. Every `state = 'firing'` reader named above is audited; a test asserts a firing-and-silenced alert\n   appears in the firing count, which is the assertion that fails today.\n5. `alerts_open_idx` states liveness without a disjunction.\n6. Comments populate `alert_events.case_id` with the alert's open case when it has one, and carry\n   `alert_id` alone when it does not — the case timeline then shows what was said during that firing.\n7. An ADR records the Alert/Case allocation with the rule that decides new cases (\"is this fact true of\n   the signal across all its firings, or only of this one firing?\"), and a gate in the shape of\n   `test/scope/forbidden_columns_test.go` enforces it against the live schema.","files":null}]}